1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,638
Friday evening again. The pipeline, the
team's automated build and test run, is red.

2
00:00:09,678 --> 00:00:14,816
The engineer on call says "the fix is
committed", and can show the commit on screen.

3
00:00:14,856 --> 00:00:17,652
And the pipeline fails
again on the same line.

4
00:00:17,992 --> 00:00:22,623
In video 1 the CTO asked three questions.
What exactly is in the commit that the

5
00:00:22,663 --> 00:00:25,834
pipeline built? Where does
that commit exist right now?

6
00:00:25,874 --> 00:00:30,680
And what is the smallest change that makes
the main branch correct, and how will we

7
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:31,848
know that it worked?

8
00:00:34,188 --> 00:00:38,866
In the last video you collected the evidence.
Now you answer all three questions, in

9
00:00:38,906 --> 00:00:40,494
order, without guessing once.

10
00:00:42,334 --> 00:00:46,335
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:46,375 --> 00:00:50,840
This video is one complete investigation,
from report to prevention.

12
00:00:50,880 --> 00:00:55,212
You have the framework from video
4 and the evidence from video 5.

13
00:00:55,252 --> 00:01:00,578
We continue with the remaining steps:
understand the state, form hypotheses, test

14
00:01:00,618 --> 00:01:05,878
them, name the root cause, select the
lowest-risk fix, execute, verify, prevent.

15
00:01:06,218 --> 00:01:10,591
Then, because first days go wrong in a
small number of known ways, we look at the

16
00:01:10,631 --> 00:01:15,004
pitfalls that the textbook collects in
section 1.13, and read what Git prints for

17
00:01:15,044 --> 00:01:19,090
each. Most of those messages name the
cause, if you read them word by word.

18
00:01:20,930 --> 00:01:22,508
Here's what you'll walk away with.

19
00:01:22,848 --> 00:01:24,260
After this video you can:

20
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:28,952
Apply the framework to the report "the fix
is committed" and find where it is not.

21
00:01:29,292 --> 00:01:31,958
List the places where "committed"
can be true or false.

22
00:01:32,298 --> 00:01:35,222
Recognize four first-day
pitfalls from their output.

23
00:01:35,562 --> 00:01:38,039
Decide when a guided
method is the wrong tool.

24
00:01:39,879 --> 00:01:44,944
Understand state. The outputs of git
rev-parse, git show and git diff from the last

25
00:01:44,984 --> 00:01:49,680
video give the content of two files in
each place. The places, once more: the

26
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:54,662
working tree is the files on disk, the
index is what the next commit will record,

27
00:01:54,702 --> 00:01:57,307
and the last commit is
the latest snapshot.

28
00:01:57,647 --> 00:02:04,510
configs/eval.yaml: in the last commit,
f29df3b, which is also on the server, it says

29
00:02:04,550 --> 00:02:10,181
timeout_s: 120. The index has the
same, and so does the working tree.

30
00:02:10,221 --> 00:02:14,701
eval/runner.py: in the last
commit, MAX_TIMEOUT_S = 60.

31
00:02:14,741 --> 00:02:18,235
In the index, 60. In
the working tree, 120.

32
00:02:18,575 --> 00:02:23,360
Say that in one sentence: the fix has two
halves, the commit holds one of them, and

33
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:28,069
the other exists only on the laptop's disk.
On screen, that's the edit sitting in

34
00:02:28,109 --> 00:02:29,406
the working tree alone.

35
00:02:29,746 --> 00:02:34,386
Form hypotheses. Four mechanisms could
produce the symptom, and for each there is

36
00:02:34,426 --> 00:02:36,177
evidence that would confirm it.

37
00:02:36,517 --> 00:02:42,434
H1: the change to runner.py was never
saved. Then the working tree would show 60.

38
00:02:42,774 --> 00:02:46,851
H2: it was saved and never
staged, so no commit contains it.

39
00:02:46,891 --> 00:02:51,381
Then the commit would show 60, and
git diff would show the change.

40
00:02:51,721 --> 00:02:56,721
H3: it was committed on another branch.
Then another commit would touch runner.py.

41
00:02:57,061 --> 00:03:01,245
H4: it was committed and not pushed,
so CI built an older commit.

42
00:03:01,285 --> 00:03:03,780
Then HEAD and origin/main would differ.

43
00:03:04,120 --> 00:03:09,666
Quick quiz, and the picture helps. Last
video's evidence already rejects one of

44
00:03:09,706 --> 00:03:14,616
these two. Option one: H3, another
branch. Option two: H4, not pushed.

45
00:03:14,656 --> 00:03:15,748
Say it out loud.

46
00:03:20,588 --> 00:03:25,639
H4. In the picture, main and origin/main
sit on the same commit, so nothing is

47
00:03:25,679 --> 00:03:31,122
waiting to be pushed. H3 is still open:
feature/cache exists, and we haven't checked

48
00:03:31,162 --> 00:03:32,166
what it touched.

49
00:03:32,506 --> 00:03:37,165
Notice that these four are the places where
"committed" can be false: not saved, not

50
00:03:37,205 --> 00:03:41,249
staged, on another branch, not pushed.
Each has its own checking command.

51
00:03:41,589 --> 00:03:46,287
Select the lowest-risk fix. Once the root
cause is named, there are two candidate

52
00:03:46,327 --> 00:03:49,445
fixes. Option one: a new
commit with the missing file.

53
00:03:49,485 --> 00:03:53,774
Its risk is 🟢: it adds a commit, and
anyone who already fetched f29df3b is

54
00:03:53,814 --> 00:03:58,688
unaffected. Option two: amend f29df3b and
force-push, which means replace the commit

55
00:03:58,728 --> 00:04:03,426
and overwrite the server's copy. That is
🟡, then 🔴: it replaces a commit that the

56
00:04:03,466 --> 00:04:08,222
server and possibly colleagues already
have. The textbook's verdict on the second:

57
00:04:08,262 --> 00:04:12,259
rejected, because a tidier log is
not worth rewriting shared history.

58
00:04:12,599 --> 00:04:17,513
Execute, with a preview. git push carries
the label 🟡 CAUTION, because it changes

59
00:04:17,553 --> 00:04:21,856
shared state: it moves a branch on
the remote and copies objects to it.

60
00:04:21,896 --> 00:04:26,994
The preview is git push --dry-run. And the
recovery line of the command-safety table

61
00:04:27,034 --> 00:04:32,070
is worth hearing once: a push can't be taken
back quietly once others have fetched.

62
00:04:32,110 --> 00:04:34,028
You publish a correcting commit.

63
00:04:34,368 --> 00:04:38,847
When not to use it. Section 1.14 lists
where Git alone is the wrong tool.

64
00:04:38,887 --> 00:04:43,986
Large binary data such as datasets, model
weights and media: every version of every

65
00:04:44,026 --> 00:04:49,062
file stays in the history and travels to
every clone, so keep a pointer in Git and

66
00:04:49,102 --> 00:04:50,238
the data elsewhere.

67
00:04:50,578 --> 00:04:53,675
Secrets: a committed and pushed
secret is in every clone.

68
00:04:53,715 --> 00:04:58,188
Deleting the file in a later commit doesn't
remove it from history, and the remedy

69
00:04:58,228 --> 00:05:02,481
starts with rotating the secret. Generated
files such as build output, virtual

70
00:05:02,521 --> 00:05:04,517
environments and caches: ignore them.

71
00:05:04,857 --> 00:05:08,982
And where the method is not enough:
the ten commands describe one clone.

72
00:05:09,022 --> 00:05:13,783
They say nothing certain about the server
until you fetch, and nothing at all about

73
00:05:13,823 --> 00:05:17,542
GitHub objects such as pull requests,
rulesets and check results.

74
00:05:19,382 --> 00:05:23,975
Hold "committed" as a chain of four links:
saved, staged, committed on the branch

75
00:05:24,015 --> 00:05:27,350
you think, pushed. A change
ships only if every link holds.

76
00:05:27,390 --> 00:05:32,097
The report "the fix is committed" asserts
the third link and says nothing about the

77
00:05:32,137 --> 00:05:32,498
others.

78
00:05:32,838 --> 00:05:38,114
The chain breaks as a model at its last
link. "Pushed" is true as far as your clone

79
00:05:38,154 --> 00:05:42,597
knows. Your clone's knowledge of the
server is the cached origin/main.

80
00:05:42,637 --> 00:05:47,913
And beyond the server there is the platform:
whether the pipeline ran, and on which

81
00:05:47,953 --> 00:05:51,308
commit, is a GitHub fact
that no local command shows.

82
00:05:53,148 --> 00:05:58,077
On the left, your clone: two commits
on main, one on feature/cache.

83
00:05:58,117 --> 00:06:03,863
Under the graph, the two lines that
matter: the index has 60 in runner.py, the

84
00:06:03,903 --> 00:06:10,094
working tree has 120. On the right, the
server has the same two commits on main, and

85
00:06:10,134 --> 00:06:15,731
CI builds f29df3b, where the constant
is 60. The push arrow carried commits.

86
00:06:15,771 --> 00:06:20,182
It didn't carry the working
tree, because a push never does.

87
00:06:20,522 --> 00:06:25,351
The root-cause box comes after the test.
Until then, hold this picture: main and

88
00:06:25,391 --> 00:06:27,238
origin/main on the same commit.

89
00:06:29,078 --> 00:06:33,900
Test the hypotheses. Three read-only
commands separate the four hypotheses.

90
00:06:33,940 --> 00:06:37,919
Predict the output of each before
it appears. Say it out loud.

91
00:06:37,959 --> 00:06:38,568
I'll wait.

92
00:06:43,408 --> 00:06:49,158
git log --all -- <path> lists the commits
on any branch that changed the path.

93
00:06:49,198 --> 00:06:54,279
Only 25fbbb0 does. So H3, "committed
on another branch", is rejected.

94
00:06:54,319 --> 00:07:00,218
git show HEAD:eval/runner.py prints the
file as the last commit recorded it: 60.

95
00:07:00,258 --> 00:07:06,008
The plain grep reads the working tree:
120. So H1, "never saved", is rejected,

96
00:07:06,048 --> 00:07:11,798
because the working tree shows 120. H4,
"not pushed", was rejected in the last

97
00:07:11,838 --> 00:07:17,959
video: HEAD and origin/main have the same
ID. H2 is confirmed: saved, never staged,

98
00:07:17,999 --> 00:07:18,924
in no commit.

99
00:07:19,264 --> 00:07:23,797
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds:
write the root cause in one sentence.

100
00:07:23,837 --> 00:07:25,931
Then compare your answer with mine.

101
00:07:30,771 --> 00:07:35,497
Root cause. The second file was never
added to the index, so it's in no commit.

102
00:07:35,537 --> 00:07:40,264
And the layer is Git. GitHub and Actions
did their job: the pipeline tested the

103
00:07:40,304 --> 00:07:41,470
commit it was given.

104
00:07:41,810 --> 00:07:47,182
Execute. git add and git commit are 🟢 SAFE.
git push is 🟡 CAUTION, so it's previewed

105
00:07:47,222 --> 00:07:50,918
with --dry-run first. Predict
what the dry run will print.

106
00:07:51,258 --> 00:07:57,286
git diff --cached now shows exactly what
the commit will record: one file, one line

107
00:07:57,326 --> 00:08:03,354
each way. The commit is 230ef10. The dry
run and the real push print the same line:

108
00:08:03,394 --> 00:08:09,349
f29df3b..230ef10 main -> main means the
server's main moved from the old commit to

109
00:08:09,389 --> 00:08:10,227
the new one.

110
00:08:10,567 --> 00:08:13,482
Verify. Repeat the commands
that showed the problem.

111
00:08:13,522 --> 00:08:16,892
The evidence must have changed
for the reason you predicted.

112
00:08:17,232 --> 00:08:20,798
Nothing tracked is modified.
The new commit is on top.

113
00:08:20,838 --> 00:08:25,406
HEAD and origin/main agree. And the
committed file has the new value.

114
00:08:25,446 --> 00:08:30,883
On GitHub, the pipeline run for 230ef10
must pass. That part can't be shown from a

115
00:08:30,923 --> 00:08:31,817
local sandbox.

116
00:08:32,157 --> 00:08:37,537
Here's the history now. 230ef10 is on top,
and the names main, origin/main and HEAD

117
00:08:37,577 --> 00:08:38,451
all sit on it.

118
00:08:38,791 --> 00:08:42,619
Prevent. Before every commit, read both
lists in git status, and read git diff

119
00:08:42,659 --> 00:08:45,198
--cached as the text of what
you're about to record.

120
00:08:45,238 --> 00:08:49,314
git commit -a would have included the second
file, because it stages every modified

121
00:08:49,354 --> 00:08:53,430
tracked file first. For the same reason it
also includes changes you didn't mean to

122
00:08:53,470 --> 00:08:55,661
commit, so it's not a
substitute for looking.

123
00:08:56,001 --> 00:08:59,574
So, the CTO's three questions.
What was in the commit?

124
00:08:59,614 --> 00:09:04,992
One half of the fix. Where is the whole
fix now? In 230ef10, on your clone and on

125
00:09:05,032 --> 00:09:10,344
the server. And how do we know? The commands
that showed the problem now show it

126
00:09:10,384 --> 00:09:10,678
gone.

127
00:09:11,018 --> 00:09:16,114
Now the first-day pitfalls. You saw one
of them, "nothing staged", in video 4.

128
00:09:16,154 --> 00:09:19,537
Here are the others. Read
each message word by word.

129
00:09:19,877 --> 00:09:21,885
The first branch is not called main.

130
00:09:22,225 --> 00:09:27,635
With no configuration at all, Git 2.55
names the first branch master and prints a

131
00:09:27,675 --> 00:09:33,219
hint. The hint says where the name comes
from, that it will change to "main" in Git

132
00:09:33,259 --> 00:09:38,871
3.0, and how to set it. The lab configuration
sets init.defaultBranch=main, which is

133
00:09:38,911 --> 00:09:44,321
why every other transcript says main. git
branch -m main is 🟡 CAUTION: it renames

134
00:09:44,361 --> 00:09:49,972
the current branch. Here the branch is
unborn, and the last line shows that only the

135
00:09:50,012 --> 00:09:51,385
text in HEAD changed.

136
00:09:51,725 --> 00:09:53,552
Git says there is no repository.

137
00:09:53,892 --> 00:09:57,014
Read the parenthesis: "or any
of the parent directories".

138
00:09:57,054 --> 00:10:01,673
Git searched the current directory and its
parents for a dot git directory and found

139
00:10:01,713 --> 00:10:02,894
none. Exit status 128.

140
00:10:03,234 --> 00:10:05,236
A repository inside a repository.

141
00:10:05,576 --> 00:10:10,088
vendor/tokenizer has its own .git
directory. Git doesn't add its files.

142
00:10:10,128 --> 00:10:15,281
It records one entry with mode 160000, a
gitlink: the ID of a commit in the inner

143
00:10:15,321 --> 00:10:20,537
repository. The warning says what follows:
clones of the outer repository will not

144
00:10:20,577 --> 00:10:25,858
contain the contents of the embedded
repository. This happens when you run git init

145
00:10:25,898 --> 00:10:30,602
or git clone inside an existing working
tree. And the hint offers an undo.

146
00:10:30,642 --> 00:10:33,487
Predict: will the command
from the hint work?

147
00:10:38,327 --> 00:10:43,541
It's refused. git rm --cached and git
restore --staged are both 🟡 CAUTION: they

148
00:10:43,581 --> 00:10:47,265
change or remove an index
entry, and files on disk stay.

149
00:10:47,305 --> 00:10:51,455
The textbook's root-cause box
gives the reason for the refusal.

150
00:10:51,495 --> 00:10:56,710
git rm refuses to drop an index entry
that matches neither HEAD nor the working

151
00:10:56,750 --> 00:10:57,042
tree.

152
00:10:57,382 --> 00:11:02,699
That test protects staged content that
exists nowhere else, and a freshly added

153
00:11:02,739 --> 00:11:08,394
embedded repository fails both halves of
it. The correct fix is git restore --staged

154
00:11:08,434 --> 00:11:11,920
vendor/tokenizer, which
resets only the index entry.

155
00:11:11,960 --> 00:11:15,785
Before the first commit there
is no HEAD to restore from.

156
00:11:16,125 --> 00:11:21,872
There, git rm --cached -f works, and
--cached guarantees that the working tree is

157
00:11:21,912 --> 00:11:27,587
not touched. The textbook notes that this
mechanism was read from the Git 2.55.0

158
00:11:27,627 --> 00:11:33,517
source. After unstaging, decide what the
inner repository is: a separate project to

159
00:11:33,557 --> 00:11:37,447
ignore, or a dependency to
add properly as a submodule.

160
00:11:39,287 --> 00:11:40,935
Five mistakes to watch for.

161
00:11:41,275 --> 00:11:46,136
A commit that lacks a file. Root cause: git
commit records the index; two files were

162
00:11:46,176 --> 00:11:47,652
edited and one was staged.

163
00:11:47,992 --> 00:11:50,998
Fixing a pushed commit by
amending and force-pushing.

164
00:11:51,038 --> 00:11:55,824
Root cause: a wish for a tidier log; the
amend replaces a commit that the server and

165
00:11:55,864 --> 00:11:57,720
possibly colleagues already have.

166
00:11:58,060 --> 00:12:03,224
"not a git repository". Root cause: Git
searched the current directory and its

167
00:12:03,264 --> 00:12:08,628
parents for .git and found none; pwd and
git rev-parse --show-toplevel show where

168
00:12:08,668 --> 00:12:09,161
you are.

169
00:12:09,501 --> 00:12:15,029
"adding embedded git repository". Root
cause: git init or git clone was run inside

170
00:12:15,069 --> 00:12:20,054
an existing working tree; run git
rev-parse --show-toplevel before either.

171
00:12:20,394 --> 00:12:23,427
Trusting the local test over
the test on the commit.

172
00:12:23,467 --> 00:12:28,154
Root cause: the local test reads the
working tree, and CI checks out the commit.

173
00:12:29,994 --> 00:12:34,742
Now, out of the lab. The case you've worked
through is the production example, and

174
00:12:34,782 --> 00:12:39,530
the textbook draws one more lesson from
it. The symptom "the fix did not ship" was

175
00:12:39,570 --> 00:12:44,026
reported against the pipeline, so the
first suspicion fell on GitHub Actions.

176
00:12:44,066 --> 00:12:48,755
The root cause was in Git, on one laptop,
in the gap between the working tree and

177
00:12:48,795 --> 00:12:49,339
the index.

178
00:12:49,679 --> 00:12:54,420
The pipeline had done what it was built to
do. For an evaluation team this matters

179
00:12:54,460 --> 00:12:58,792
twice: a score computed from a working
tree with uncommitted edits can't be

180
00:12:58,832 --> 00:13:03,689
reproduced from the commit ID in the report.
Trust the test that runs on the commit.

181
00:13:05,529 --> 00:13:12,203
Your turn. Do Exercise 1.9, Level 4,
"git log says there are no commits", in

182
00:13:12,243 --> 00:13:15,118
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

183
00:13:15,458 --> 00:13:20,279
Before you run anything in the generated
sandbox, write the symptom in one sentence

184
00:13:20,319 --> 00:13:24,788
without interpretation, and list at least
three hypotheses with the read-only

185
00:13:24,828 --> 00:13:28,360
command that would separate them.
Only then start the ritual.

186
00:13:30,200 --> 00:13:34,843
Q11: "A colleague says "the fix is committed".
List the distinct places where that

187
00:13:34,883 --> 00:13:38,611
statement can be true or false,
and the command that checks each."

188
00:13:40,951 --> 00:13:45,639
Answer out loud. A strong answer walks the
path of a change from the editor to the

189
00:13:45,679 --> 00:13:50,368
machine that builds it, names each place
the change can be missing, and pairs each

190
00:13:50,408 --> 00:13:55,096
place with one read-only command. It
distinguishes what your clone knows from what

191
00:13:55,136 --> 00:13:59,940
the server holds now. Don't open the answers
file until you've named every place you

192
00:13:59,980 --> 00:14:00,690
can think of.

193
00:14:02,530 --> 00:14:07,926
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say: "committed" can be false in four

194
00:14:07,966 --> 00:14:12,071
places: not saved, not staged,
on another branch, not pushed.

195
00:14:12,111 --> 00:14:17,574
I separate those with git diff, git show
HEAD:<path>, git log --all -- <path> and

196
00:14:17,614 --> 00:14:19,681
git rev-parse HEAD origin/main.

197
00:14:20,021 --> 00:14:24,578
When the faulty commit is already shared,
I add a commit and don't rewrite.

198
00:14:24,618 --> 00:14:29,666
I preview a push with --dry-run, and I
verify by repeating the commands that showed

199
00:14:29,706 --> 00:14:34,632
the problem. And when Git prints a message,
I read it word by word, including the

200
00:14:34,672 --> 00:14:37,819
hints, without assuming that
a hint is always right.

201
00:14:39,659 --> 00:14:43,676
Read sections 1.12 to 1.15 of Chapter 1.

202
00:14:44,016 --> 00:14:46,646
Do the Practice section 1.17.

203
00:14:46,986 --> 00:14:53,732
Challenge: Exercise 1.8, Level 3, "the
object that no history shows", in

204
00:14:53,772 --> 00:14:56,842
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

205
00:14:57,182 --> 00:15:02,438
That was a complete investigation, from a
red pipeline to a prevention rule, and you

206
00:15:02,478 --> 00:15:06,095
never had to guess. Do the
exercise before the next video.

207
00:15:06,135 --> 00:15:10,571
Next time, Part 1 begins: snapshots,
not diffs, and content addressing.

208
00:15:10,611 --> 00:15:13,850
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

209
00:15:13,890 --> 00:15:15,363
See you in the next one.
